{
  "slug": "binary-tree-zigzag-level-order-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Zigzag Level Order Traversal",
  "topic": "trees",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "Level-order BFS with alternating direction",
  "statement": "A binary tree is stored in an array using the implicit layout: index 0 holds the root, and the node stored at index i has its left child at index 2*i + 1 and its right child at index 2*i + 2 whenever those indices are still inside the array. Every index of the array is a node, index 0 sits on level 0, and every other node sits one level below its parent.\n\nWalk the levels from the top down. Write the nodes of level 0 from left to right, the nodes of level 1 from right to left, the nodes of level 2 from left to right, and keep flipping the direction for each next level.\n\nReturn an array of levels, where each level is an array of the node values written in the direction that level requires. Nodes of a level are considered left to right in increasing index order.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= tree.length <= 200` and `-100 <= tree[i] <= 100`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "tree = [-100]",
      "output": "[[-100]]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "tree = [-5, 95, -59, 38, 99]",
      "output": "[[-5], [-59, 95], [38, 99]]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(tree: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Direction changes per level, but the nodes of a level never change. Build each level in left-to-right order first, then flip it if this level needs the opposite direction.",
    "Level d of an implicit array sits at the indices you reach after d rounds of expanding children 2*i + 1 and 2*i + 2, so an array of indices is all the state you need to carry between levels.",
    "Keep a frontier of indices, read its values for the answer, reverse the copy on odd levels, and generate the next frontier from the frontier itself, which is already in increasing index order."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Both versions carry one array of indices that represents the current level and process the tree level by level. The optimal version emits the level's values in index order, reverses the emitted copy when the level number is odd, and builds the next level from the children of each index. Children arrive in increasing index order for free, because the left and right child of an index i are 2*i + 1 and 2*i + 2 and the levels are processed left to right, so the next level always starts out correctly ordered and only the odd levels need their copy reversed. The brute force keeps the same level structure but finds the children the hard way. Instead of computing them, it walks every index j of the array once per node of the current level and keeps j whenever (j - 1) / 2 equals that node, which costs O(n) work per node and O(n\u00b2) overall.",
    "why_optimal": "Every index belongs to exactly one level and must appear exactly once in the output, so all n values have to be copied at least once, which makes O(n) time the floor for this problem. The optimal version hits that floor: each index is read once when its level is emitted and once when its children are generated, and the reversals only touch the levels that need flipping. The O(n) space is the returned output plus the frontier, and a level of an implicitly stored tree can hold about half of all nodes. The brute force adds a factor of n because it re-derives the children of each node from the entire array instead of from the index arithmetic.",
    "pitfalls": "Reversing without keeping the original left-to-right order first, which corrupts the next level, since the children must be generated from the level in index order rather than in the order it was printed. Reading the values of a level straight from an index range instead of from the frontier, which silently assumes the array is a perfect binary tree and fails on any other length. Reversing the wrong parity, which flips the whole pattern, and reversing the emitted level in place when the same array is still being used to produce children. An array of length 1 must produce a single level, and length 0 must produce an empty result."
  },
